1b. Other proprietary vendor device-API files
'''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''

These converter drivers are BSD 3-Clause except for the vendor device-API
files listed here, which are proprietary under the ADI "Software License
Agreement" ("This software is proprietary to Analog Devices, Inc. and its
licensors"), the same non-permissive family as the CTSLA in 1a.

  Proprietary files                                   License
  --------------------------------------------------- ---------------------
  drivers/adc/ad9083/ad9083_api/                      ADI "Software
  drivers/adc/ad9208/ad9208_api/                      License Agreement"
  drivers/dac/ad917x/ad917x_api/                      (proprietary,
                                                      non-permissive)

  The ADI "API license" (Cross-Technology Source Code Software License
  Agreement, "CTSLA") and the ADI "Software License Agreement" are
  proprietary licenses. Among other conditions, they do NOT permit the
  covered software to be combined or merged with other software in a way
  that would subject it to differing terms. Read the referenced LICENSE
  file, or the file's own header, before redistributing.

1c. Files under a different permissive license
''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''

A few files are permissively licensed but under BSD-2-Clause or MIT
rather than the BSD-3-Clause default. The license is stated in each
file's own header:

  Path                                      License        Notes
  ----------------------------------------- -------------- ----------------
  drivers/adc/ltc2312/                      BSD-2-Clause   third-party
                                                           (Linear
                                                           Technology)
  tools/scripts/platform/xilinx/util.py     BSD-2-Clause   Analog Devices
  drivers/potentiometer/ad5293/             MIT            third-party
                                                           (Ming Zeng)

  BSD-2-Clause is permissive and BSD-3-Clause compatible; it simply omits
  the third ("no endorsement") clause of the no-OS default. MIT is also
  permissive and BSD-3-Clause compatible.

1d. Vendor device-API files under GPL-2.0
'''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''

The AD9081 vendor device-API tree is copyleft, licensed GPL-2.0, matching
the same code as carried in the Linux kernel. The SPDX tag is stated in
each file's own header; the surrounding driver and no-OS wrapper remain
BSD 3-Clause.

  Driver tree                               License        Notes
  ----------------------------------------- -------------- ----------------
  drivers/adc/ad9081/api/                   GPL-2.0        vendor device
                                                           API, as in the
                                                           Linux kernel

  GPL-2.0 is copyleft: a binary that links this code must, as a whole, be
  distributable under the GPL-2.0. Build the AD9081 driver only if that is
  acceptable for your combination.

1e. The JESD204 framework (GPL-2.0+)
''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''''

Unlike the optional vendor code in 1a-1d, the JESD204 framework is a core
no-OS subsystem ported from the Linux kernel. Like the kernel original it
is GPL-2.0+ (GPL-2.0 or, at your option, any later version), stated by the
SPDX tag in each file.

  Path                                      License        Notes
  ----------------------------------------- -------------- ----------------
  jesd204/                                  GPL-2.0+       ported from the
  include/jesd204.h                                        Linux kernel

  This matters because JESD204 is not an optional device driver: any
  project that uses the framework links GPL-2.0+ code, so the resulting
  combination must be distributable under the GPL. Projects that do not
  use JESD204 are unaffected.


2. Example projects not under BSD 3-Clause
------------------------------------------

These example projects build against the non-permissive RF-transceiver
drivers above and are themselves under the same terms (ADI "API license"
/ CTSLA and GPL-2.0, per file; see the file headers). They are optional
and are not part of the linkable library.

  projects/ad9371/     projects/adrv9001/   projects/adrv9009/
  projects/adrv902x/   projects/adrv903x/   projects/adrv904x/

A few individual files in other example projects carry a different
permissive license, stated in the file header:

  projects/eval-ade9430/src/examples/iot_example/    MIT
    iot_sample_common.{c,h}


3. Bundled third-party libraries
--------------------------------

Each library under libraries/ is third-party code bundled for
convenience and is governed by its own LICENSE / COPYING file in its
directory. It is only built when a project selects it. None is under the
no-OS BSD 3-Clause default.

  Library                                   License
  ----------------------------------------- ---------------------
  libraries/free_rtos/                      MIT
  libraries/azure/                          MIT
  libraries/lvgl/                           MIT
  libraries/mbedtls/                        Apache-2.0
  libraries/lwip/                           BSD
  libraries/fatfs/                          FatFs (BSD-like)
  libraries/pico-sdk/                       BSD-3-Clause
  libraries/mqtt/                           EPL-1.0 / EDL-1.0
  libraries/precision-converters-library/   see its LICENSE
  libraries/esh/, libraries/tmc/            see their LICENSE


4. How to determine the license of a specific file
--------------------------------------------------

The authoritative license for any file is the one declared in that file
itself, either via an SPDX-License-Identifier tag or a license header.
The tables above are a navigation aid, not a substitute for the file's
own terms. Where a file header and this document appear to disagree, the
file header governs.


Machine-readable license data
-----------------------------

The full text of every license used by Analog Devices' own no-OS code is
collected under the LICENSES/ directory, one file per license, grouped by
role (preferred, permissive, copyleft, proprietary). The
top-level COPYING file is a short pointer to the default license and to
that directory. The rules for how source files declare their license, and
which licenses are acceptable for new contributions, are described in
doc/sphinx/source/license_rules.rst.


A note on aggregation
---------------------

The mere aggregation of these files (putting them side by side in the
same source code repository or on a hard disk) does not create one
master license for all the files. It is up to you, the user, to ensure
that when you build a project which combines these files, all the
individual licenses are compatible. For example, if a single file in the
combination is covered by the GPL, the whole combination must be
distributed under the GPL; if you cannot or will not do that, you may
not distribute the resulting binary. Some of the proprietary ADI "API
license" trees additionally forbid such combination outright.

Nothing in this file restricts, limits, or otherwise affects any rights
or obligations you may have, or conditions to which you may be subject,
under the license terms of any particular file. This document does not
limit your rights under, or grant you rights that supersede, the license
terms of any particular file.
